you wait two years for a Mars mission  and then three come along at once there  are three separate spacecraft all  heading to Mars this year from the  United States China and the United Arab  Emirates but the middle of a pandemic  hardly seems like the best time to plan  a launch so why now one reason the teams  are all blasting off at about the same  time is that the next few months fall  within a perfect launch window right  about now Earth and Mars are lining up  in a way that makes it much easier than  usual to fly a vehicle from one planet  to the other this ideal alignment  happens only once every 2.2 years but  why 2020 and not 2018 or 2022 to answer  that question we need to take a look at  the journeys each of the three countries  has taken to get to this point for NASA  at the United States Space Agency this  is the fifth Rover to have sent to the  surface of the Red Planet the first  Sojourner landed in 1997 it was the size  of a microwave and made it just a  hundred meters across the surface the  latest rover Curiosity arrived on Mars  in 2012 and is still exploring the new  Rover is called perseverance it's almost  identical and designed to curiosity but  with one big difference it's designed to  drill and collect rock samples from the  Red Planet  that can hopefully one day be brought  back to earth and analyzed for signs of  ancient life so for NASA 2020 is the  culmination of decades of work and if  they don't launch now they'll face big  costs for delaying it's also their best  chance of being the first team to bring  Martian rock to earth soil China may be  behind the u.s. in terms of direct Mars  experience but they've had several space  successes in the last few years in 2013  they became the third country to land a  spacecraft on the moon and in 2019 the  first to land on the moon's far side  their lunar landers provide a template  for the rover they're sending to Mars as  part of the tn1 one  Mars however is a much longer trip and  potentially a much bigger challenge so  success would be a major triumph so  after years of slowly and methodically  building up their space program for  China the 2020 mission is the next  logical step towards demonstrating there  a major space power even a pandemic  won't get in the way of this important  goal finally the youngest space agency  joining the trip belongs to the United  Arab Emirates they started their space  program just six years ago with the aim  of sending their hope orbiter to map the  Martian weather it's a hugely ambitious  project that will be the first  interplanetary adventure of any Arab  nation many people hope it will open up  new areas of industry in the UAE and  inspire young people to become  scientists and for the UAE the 2020  launch window is their only opportunity  when the government set up the space  agency they gave them a deadline for  getting to Mars it had to be before the  country's 50th anniversary celebrations  in December 2021 so for the Emirati Mars  mission it's now or never  ultimately for all the teams it came  down to scheduling this is the moment  that the funding and the project  timelines and the planets all aligned  for these three countries their success  is by no means guaranteed Mars is a  notorious graveyard for failed  spacecraft and the ongoing pandemic will  undoubtedly create extra challenges to  overcome  but if perseverance tn1 and hope do make  it safely to Mars they could rewrite our  understanding of this enticing and  mysterious planet
 

